  </p>

  <h2>Why “just post on Indeed” fails for trades</h2>
  <p>
    Indeed is built for volume applications. Trades hiring needs volume of <em>serious, skilled</em>
    people, helpers, journeymen, electrician sales roles, who will finish a multi-step process.
    Soft listings attract soft applicants. You pay for clicks and résumés; your week fills with
    no-shows.
  </p>
  <div class="receipt">
    <div class="receipt-label">THE INDEED RECEIPT</div>
    <p>
      One electrical company we work with spent over $1,000 on Indeed and got nobody worth
      interviewing. The same shop later ran a Facebook sprint and had interview conversations
      moving within days. (The final hire path can still include referrals, the point is the
      channel that produces candidates, not a fairy tale that ads replace judgment.)
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    That pattern shows up enough that we stopped treating job boards as the default for
    <a href="/services/hiring">home service recruiting</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What a Facebook hiring sprint looks like for electricians</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Role intake with the owner.</strong> Helpers, journeymen, sales, pay mix, commission,
      non-negotiables. You set the bar.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Meta campaigns that speak to upside.</strong> Average messaging attracts average
      applicants. If you’ll pay for excellence, say so clearly.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Skill-first screening.</strong> We filter for the skills you defined. Culture and gut
      feel stay with you in person, we’re not hiring your shop from New Zealand.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Multi-step seriousness filter.</strong> Who finishes the process is the signal.
      Reliability shows up before the interview.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Interview slots you control.</strong> You give windows; we fill them with
      pre-qualified people.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Typical timing: talking to people by about day three; enough strong candidates by about day ten
    that owners pause spend. Strict roles take longer. Fluff roles don’t.
  </p>

  <h2>Who actually used this?</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/case-studies/rewired">Rewired</a> grew from 5 to 12 crew while moving from roughly
    $60k to $220k+ months, Facebook hiring for helpers, apprentices, and salespeople was part of
    owning the full stack, not a side project.
    <a href="/case-studies/honest-hanks">Honest Hank’s</a> went from one tech toward three while
    revenue climbed $25k → $80k months; growth without hiring is how vans sit empty.
    <a href="/case-studies/dry-duck">Dry Duck</a> staffed into demand the same way as the brand
    scaled.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
